// A free function whose name collides with a class must not overwrite that
// class's constructor. A class publishes its constructor under the bare class
// name (restoreProcedureConstructorAliases) and lookupProcedure() follows aliases
// transparently, so defining `widget` after `class Widget` used to rebind
// widget.widget's bytecode address to the free function's body: `new Widget(5)`
// then ran the free function and left count at its default 0.
//
// Declaration order matters to the mechanism -- the alias only exists once the
// class has been seen -- so the collision is declared after the class here, which
// is the ordering that was broken.
//
// Scope note: this fixture pins constructor integrity only. Calling a free
// function through a name that collides with a class is a separate, still-rough
// area of Rea (a two-argument call does not even parse), so `widget` is
// deliberately declared and not called.
class Widget { int count; Widget(int v) { my.count = v; } }

void widget(Widget t) { writeln("free fn ran - constructor was clobbered"); }

Widget w = new Widget(5);
writeln(w.count);
